 @Tasteful_Stupidity  the vram on the card has to be a factor of the bus width, generally in production of chips it's cheaper to make a chip and cut it in half like they have here, and cutting the chip in half means half the bus width so the only options are 8gb or 16gb (outside of funky vram chips which are more expensive, looking at you 1080ti)
Hope that makes sense :)
(And I don't disagree that it's really dumb, just make a card with a different bus width, you can half the 24gb gpu chip or something)

 @PaperReaper  Basically every game support DLSS and this allows the latest upscaling methods that scale way better than competitors and allows using the best denoising by a mile. If you run FSR or XeSS cards, they have a massive limitation because such a tiny percentage of all games support their latest native AI upscaling. Even when supported, they still scale worse with a lower image quality. Benchmarks don't ever show the difference in real world image quality differences and what is needed to match all the cards. Why this annoys me, I also run latest model AMD and Intel secondary cards/systems. Such a massive user experience difference, because the same tech/options isn't available on a vast amount of titles. This can easily break the experience and performance. Been there way too often.

This is just one example. The RT will be game dependant. Mostly because some games are built for RT natively and have a massive impact. Pick any game and the one company card performs better with higher image quality. Newer games are also designed to use upscaling tech because of TAA, so Nvidia is the obvious pick. It does not only offer the upscaling, but also the DLAA and DLDSR options. Even on native screen resolution, RTX cards still offer the best performance and visuals. Users simply have more native options and things just work. Pick any game, you'll always have more options. The difference is even bigger for people running ultrawide, 4k, high refresh rate screens. 

These are just some examples, but there are so many software features that users can manage. Not even talking about card designs on multiple generations. All RTX cards have the same base functions, apps, and features. With Intel and AMD, this is all over the place.

 @PaperReaper  FSR doesn't mean it support AI upscaling methods. The exact reason why I said only a small % supports it. The game needs to run FSR 3.1 or FSR 4 to enable the proper version. This is the whole reason why AMD have such an issue on their hand. Intel with XeSS 2.0 have the similar problem. Great tech, such a minimal game support. You can't even compare the older FSR image quality against latest DLSS, the difference is on another level. Like I said on my comment, the native looks even way better on Nvidia card because of DLAA and DLDSR. New games with the native TAA looks horrible, so why would you ever pick it over DLAA. If you run your games on a 1080p monitor, you really want to use DLDSR because it offers insane visual quality, or at least the DLAA.

Full HD popularity is mostly because of the laptop and E-sport title market on Steam surveys. Almost all of those gaming laptops are Nvidia GPUs, and the performance doesn't make much of a difference on non-demanding titles. If you check what resolution monitors average AAA game enjoyer buys, those are 1440p and 4k nowadays, plus people who play on their 4k TVs. All these not only require upscaling tech, but higher screen resolution is better pick, because you can render games on lower resolution and still get better image quality. Those who buy a new gen at least mid-tier card, they are playing with a higher resolution. The only exception are the online FPS gamers.

With RT, even the latest models are behind on performance and most importantly, software features that manage the image quality for the RT.

 @PaperReaper  Now that's something that have no effect for the TAA usage on game development side. Since the TAA have been used basically every demanding game for a long time now, you'll automatically get the better image quality with DLAA or even DLSS. You totally missed my comment if you didn't get this. DLAA means it uses the native screen resolution, but is still better than TAA. Or even better, use the DLDSR. FSR 4 and XeSS 2 also require TAA support, so games will be made for those in mind.

Before you were commenting about the performance difference, now it's all about avoiding monopoly. My comment was purely what's the best tech for gamers. It doesn't make any difference if you or me pick any GPU. I make much bigger difference for sending the actual feedback straight to companies after test sessions with every new card generation. I've been doing this for 12 years straight for Nvidia, AMD, and now even Intel cards. Every time I added my detailed suggestions of what improvements those GPUs need in the future. AMD did the opposite basically every single time. They lack any long term vision for all their cards and do zero innovation on GPU side. 

I think the change would happen faster if AMD and Intel users would do the same and not ignoring those issues. Not buying an Nvidia card doesn't mean AMD or Intel would spend more on software development, innovation, and change their views on day 1 game support.

 @PaperReaper  Do you realize your earlier comments harms more your cause than anything else. Trying to push the narrative that the raster difference is all that matters and sending others the faulty positive image of products and features, does no good. The only thing to battle against Nvidia is to spend more on the software development and creating a solid long term plan. AMD or Intel can't ever take over without these. 

Forget your anger towards Nvidia and start thinking how other companies can offer the similar features or something better. If they want to do better, they need a lot of innovations like they do on the CPU market. In the last 10 years, there has been no GPU innovations on the AMD side. At minimum, they need to have every single card with AI tech close to DLSS 4 level, and day one game support for that tech. This mean adding budget for game studios and not relying on, “they will add it someday”. EU won't ever do anything about software features, because those aren't mandatory for products to work. It's easier to push the competition by demanding better products with better features. 

Buying products that lack the features just show the company they don't have to do anything else. AMD did this all because they made wrong decisions every time over 10 years, not because Nvidia did scummy things. You can only win if the company starts making things right every generation forward.

 @PaperReaper  You say like it's so easy, but you didn't mention how it could be done. EU as a whole needs Nvidia way more than Nvidia needs EU. There's no real power to force Nvidia to open up their software side. Nvidia would leave the EU market instantly if they would be forces to open source their software. The one side have all the power, and it's not the EU. This isn't some charging port case or minor legal change.

What do you think would happen in a scenario where the EU forced some blatantly illegal move to regulate their dedicated first party software? If one company gets similar  punishment and not every company in similar position, it would crash the economy. In reality, massive amount of companies would instantly stop doing any business inside the EU. Why would any software company open their insanely expensive tech that they have R&D for billions of dollars. Companies whose business rely on Nvidia tech would take instant action to fight against the EU. This might be something that would end the whole EU, because non-EU European countries could take all the major data center projects and keep doing business uninterrupted. 

If EU forced Nvidia to leave the EU market, it would literally crash the stock market, large scale sale stoppage, destroy a large percentage of companies who have built their systems for Nvidia hardware. Every field I can think of uses Nvidia in some ways. Google, MS, Amazon, META, Oracle,  car industry, teleoperation, streaming platforms, entertainment, game studios, research projects, space industries, universities, etc.  It would also stop pretty much all the AI datacenter projects in the EU. Like I said, EU has no power to enforce such insane changes. No one with a working brain wouldn't even push such regulation.

